{"reference":"Job 21","book":"Job","chapter":21,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Wider Debate","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 21 belongs to the sustained debate that follows the friends’ initial seven days of silent presence. As the discussion continues, Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar increasingly treat Job’s suffering as evidence of wrongdoing, while Job rejects that diagnosis. This background clarifies the sharpness of his demand that they listen and his charge that their comfort is empty: he is resisting an explanation that turns suffering into proof of guilt.","verse_refs":[],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_021_7fa710a87893069d3cde6fb5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Some interpreters connect Job’s story with later language about endurance or steadfastness. In that reading, steadfastness describes his persistence through suffering and the story’s eventual outcome; it should not be taken to require silent acceptance of abuse or to forbid seeking safety and protection.","verse_refs":[],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_021_52128d8c99a9b42f1c79f605"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}